WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:10.630
- What are your thoughts on the efforts

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:13.150
of the new folks,

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:15.820
SpaceX and Elon Musk?

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:17.820
What are your thoughts
about what Elon is doing?

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.600
Do you see him as competition?

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.153
Do you enjoy competition?

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:23.173
What are your thoughts?

00:00:23.173 --> 00:00:24.353
- Yeah, first of all,

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.220
certainly Elon, I would say SpaceX,

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:30.170
and some of those other ventures

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:32.440
are definitely a competitive force

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:34.760
in the space industry.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:36.860
And, do we like competition?

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:37.860
Yeah, we do.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:42.490
And, we think we're
very strong competitors.

00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:43.439
I think it's,

00:00:43.439 --> 00:00:47.770
competition is what the U.S. is founded on

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:49.030
in a lot of ways,

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:51.610
and always coming up with a better way.

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:56.410
And I think it's really
important to continue

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:59.910
to have fresh eyes coming
in, new innovation.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:02.490
I do think it's important to
have level playing fields,

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:05.050
and so you want to make sure that,

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:08.150
that you're not giving
different requirements

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:09.770
to different players.

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:12.150
But, you know, I tell people,

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:13.310
and, you know, I spend a lot of time

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:14.480
at places like MIT.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:17.550
I'm gonna be at the MIT
Beaver Works Summer Institute

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:18.760
over the weekend here.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:22.000
And I tell people this
is the most exciting time

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.350
to be in the space
business in my entire life.

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:29.569
And, it is this explosion
of new capabilities

00:01:29.569 --> 00:01:32.630
that have been driven by things like

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:35.169
the massive increase in computing power,

00:01:35.169 --> 00:01:39.980
things like the massive
increase in comms capabilities,

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:42.080
advanced and additive manufacturing,

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:45.750
are really bringing down
the barriers to entry

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:48.850
in this field, and it's driving
just incredible innovation.

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:49.990
And, it's happening at start-ups,

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:52.510
but it's also happening
at Lockheed Martin.

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:54.310
I talked about some of
these, like, you know,

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:57.310
Maya and Orion, but we're in the middle

00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:59.570
of what we call SmartSats

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:01.320
and software defined satellites

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.230
that can essentially restructure and remap

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:07.820
their purpose, their mission on orbit

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:11.010
to give you almost unlimited flexibility

00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:13.400
for these satellites over their lifetimes.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.630
So, those are just a couple of examples,

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:18.563
but, yeah, this is a
great time to be in space.

